    <!--
      Ninebot legacy pairing warnings.

      These are not optional niceties. Pairing a legacy Ninebot scooter requires
      a PHYSICAL power-button press, and the scooter stores only ONE paired
      client — so registering this app evicts the official vendor app, and
      re-pairing that app will evict this one. Neither is something the rider can
      work around, so both must be stated before they commit, not reported as an
      error afterwards. See doc/NINEBOT_LEGACY_PROTOCOL.md §5.
    -->
    <string name="pairing_power_button_title">Press the scooter\'s power button</string>
    <string name="pairing_power_button_body">The scooter is waiting for you to physically press its power button. A pairing attempt that appears to hang is usually waiting for this.</string>
    <string name="pairing_single_client_title">This replaces your existing pairing</string>
    <string name="pairing_single_client_body">This scooter can only remember one paired app. Pairing here will disconnect it from the official vendor app, which you will then need to re-pair. Re-pairing the vendor app will in turn disconnect this one.</string>
    <string name="pairing_continue">I understand, pair</string>

    <!-- Shown when a scooter answers but its protocol dialect is unknown. -->
    <string name="protocol_unknown_title">Unrecognised scooter</string>
    <string name="protocol_unknown_body">This scooter answered, but not in any protocol this app knows. Support for it has not been verified, so no readings will be shown.</string>

    <!--
      Shown for a scooter whose protocol IS recognised but whose register layout
      is not published — current-generation models (G2 / F2 / D-series) over
      Encryption2, and the Ninebot legacy family.

      The wording matters. This is not a failure the rider can retry away, and it
      must not read like one: the app is deliberately showing nothing rather than
      showing guessed numbers. A community integration that guessed produced
      "1924.9 km range" and "1387.5 km/h" — the ASCII bytes of a vehicle
      identifier read as telemetry. See doc/NINEBOT_LEGACY_PROTOCOL.md and the
      module docs of ninebot-ble/src/encryption2.rs.
    -->
    <string name="telemetry_unsupported_title">Connected, but readings are unavailable</string>
    <string name="telemetry_unsupported_body">This scooter uses a protocol we understand, but its register layout has not been published. We show no readings rather than guessing at them, because a wrong number is harder to notice than a missing one.</string>

